WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:06.929
Let's discuss the features and tools within Sequencer and how they can impact your speed and efficiency when using them.

2
00:00:07.769 --> 00:00:12.390
Working our way numerically from one to five, let's cover the Sequencer interface.

3
00:00:12.869 --> 00:00:19.309
In section one, this is the toolbar, which is a collection of commonly used tools and settings within Sequencer.

4
00:00:19.649 --> 00:00:22.429
We have the option to save the sequence,

5
00:00:23.109 --> 00:00:29.550
browse to the sequence within the content browser, create a new camera and set it as the current camera cut,

6
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:34.689
export the sequence to a movie or image sequence via movie render queue,

7
00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.759
open the blueprint director for the sequence,

8
00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:45.920
execute various actions. Now, note many of these actions are available in the interface or with hotkeys.

9
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:50.719
Change your interface layout and the information that's displayed and

10
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.600
modify your playback options.

11
00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.299
Now the next three menu options are some of the most commonly used when working with Sequencer.

12
00:00:59.619 --> 00:01:06.040
The first option gives the ability to control default keying channels as well as keyframe interpolation.

13
00:01:06.459 --> 00:01:13.640
The next option is auto keyframe, which will automatically create a key when parameters have changed.

14
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.280
A sequencer will only create a key if a previous key has been created.

15
00:01:19.799 --> 00:01:28.719
And lastly, we can restrict Sequencer's ability to automatically create keys for properties when they're modified within the sequence, the level or both.

16
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.890
The viewport selectability button limits your ability to choose actors within the viewport.

17
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:39.200
When this button is active, you can only select objects using the sequence.

18
00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:45.000
You can disable snapping or enable it as well as change the snapping interval.

19
00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:47.849
You could modify the frame rate of the sequence.

20
00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:52.890
Finally, you can use the curve editor for granular control over your keyframes.

21
00:01:55.519 --> 00:02:00.959
Section 2 is the outliner. Actors and their properties that are part of the sequence are displayed in this section.

22
00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:08.360
Note, typically only some of the actors' keyable properties will be shown here when the actor is added to the sequence.

23
00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:12.960
You can add more keyable properties using the add button, the plus button,

24
00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:20.479
located on the actors section or by adding a keyframe to the actors properties from a detail panel.

25
00:02:23.219 --> 00:02:28.240
Section 3 is our timeline. As the playhead advances, it will progress through the various keyframes,

26
00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:31.699
triggers, and events based on their position on the timeline.

27
00:02:33.389 --> 00:02:39.430
Section 4 is the selection tab, which can be docked or the default fly-out,

28
00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:44.389
and contains many of the same properties displayed when right-clicking an actor in the sequence.

29
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.000
Lastly, section 5 contains our playback controls,

30
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:56.719
buttons to specify our in and out points for the sequence or shot, and our timeline zoom and view options.

31
00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:04.840
Now recent updates to Sequencer allows you to filter tracks based on your outliner selections.

32
00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:09.960
You can isolate only the selected tracks, hide selected tracks, or display all.

33
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:19.080
After selecting a track or tracks, the sequencer curves editor can also be used to manipulate keyframes in keyframe interpolations.

34
00:03:19.479 --> 00:03:25.280
Now across the top of the sequencer curve editor window are various features for displaying,

35
00:03:26.159 --> 00:03:29.240
manipulating, scaling, and snapping your keyframes.

36
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.599
Additionally, you have the ability to set the keyframe interpolations as well as the in and out timings for those keyframes.

37
00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:46.319
The add level sequence with shots tool is useful for automatically generating a master sequence that includes a structured hierarchy of

38
00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:52.680
nested sequences and associated sequences all within a highly organized folder structure.

39
00:03:53.259 --> 00:03:58.860
While you'll most likely find that this tool doesn't work perfectly for your established pipeline or

40
00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:00.819
desired folder structure,

41
00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:07.139
I highly recommend you play with it at least once to observe the way Unreal creates the parent sequence,

42
00:04:07.699 --> 00:04:11.740
nested child sequences, and the file and folder structures.
